Source Question: Underline the changes in the field of society and economy from the Rig Vedic to the later Vedic period. [Year: 2024] [Marks: 10]

The transition from the Rig Vedic (1500-1000 BCE) to the Later Vedic period (1000-600 BCE) witnessed fundamental changes in society and economy as the Vedic Aryans shifted from the Indus-Sapta Sindhu region to the Gangetic plains.

**Changes in Society**

| Rig Vedic Period | Later Vedic Period |
| --- | --- |
| Varna system was flexible and<br>occupation-based. Eg- Purusha Sukta<br>mentions varnas but social mobility existed. | Varna became rigid and birth-based. Brahmins and<br>Kshatriyas gained dominance. Shudras were<br>increasingly marginalised. |
| Women participated in assemblies and had<br>access to education. Eg- Gargi, Maitreyi,<br>Lopamudra were scholar-poetesses. | Women's status declined - excluded from political<br>assemblies, subjected to early marriage, and lost<br>property rights. |
| Monogamy was common; women had<br>choice in marriage (Swayamvara existed). | Polygamy became more common among elites. Gotra<br>system became important for regulating marriages. |
| Tribal polity - Jan (tribe) and Vis (clan) were<br>primary units. Sabha and Samiti were<br>democratic assemblies. | Rise of territorial kingdoms (Janapadas). Sabha and<br>Samiti declined. Elaborate coronation rituals like<br>Rajasuya and Vajapeya emerged. |

**Changes in Economy**

| Rig Vedic Period | Later Vedic Period |
| --- | --- |
| Predominantly pastoral economy - cattle (Go)<br>was the measure of wealth. Eg- Wars were<br>called ‘Gavishthi’ (search for cows). | Agriculture became the mainstay - use of iron<br>ploughshare (Krishnayasa) enabled clearing of<br>Gangetic forests. |
| No concept of private ownership of land. | Land became an important economic resource. King<br>claimed a share of agricultural produce (Bali). |
| --- | --- |
| Limited crafts - carpentry, weaving, pottery. | Diversification of crafts - metalwork, pottery (PGW -<br>Painted Grey Ware), leather work. Trade expanded<br>with use of Nishka (gold ornament used as currency). |
| Semi-nomadic, shifting settlements along<br>river banks. | Permanent settlements and proto-urban centres<br>emerged in the Gangetic plains. Eg- Hastinapur,<br>Kaushambi. |

The transition from the Rig Vedic to the Later Vedic period marked India's evolution from a pastoral-tribal society to a settled agricultural civilisation, laying the foundations for the rise of Mahajanapadas and the Second Urbanisation.

