The Gandhian era (1920-1947) witnessed unprecedented diversification of India's freedom struggle, transforming it from an elite movement into a mass-based national awakening representing all sections of society.

## Mass Participation Across Social Groups

-** Peasant Movements **-** Champaran Satyagraha (1917)**: Gandhi's first major success mobilizing indigo farmers against exploitative plantation system
 -** Kheda Satyagraha (1918)**: United farmers against excessive land revenue during crop failure
 -** Bardoli Satyagraha (1928)**: Sardar Patel led successful tax resistance, demonstrating organized peasant power
 -** All India Kisan Sabha (1936)**: Provided unified platform for agrarian demands and rural mobilization
 -** Tebhaga Movement (Bengal)**: Sharecroppers' struggle integrated with broader nationalist goals

-** Working Class Movements **-** All India Trade Union Congress (1920)**: N.M. Joshi and Lala Lajpat Rai linked labor issues with nationalism
 -** Ahmedabad Mill Strike (1918)**: Gandhi's first industrial satyagraha established worker-nationalist alliance
 -** Railway Strike (1974)**: Mass participation of railway workers across India
 -** Textile workers in Bombay and Ahmedabad**: Regular strikes supporting nationalist calls
 - Integration of** economic demands with political freedom **strengthened movement base

## Women's Revolutionary Participation

-** Sarojini Naidu**: First Indian woman Congress President, led Salt Satyagraha
-** Kasturba Gandhi**: Symbolized women's sacrifice, imprisoned multiple times
-** Aruna Asaf Ali**: Hoisted tricolor during Quit India Movement, underground resistance leader
-** Usha Mehta**: Operated Secret Congress Radio during 1942 movement
-** Captain Lakshmi Sahgal**: Led women's regiment in Indian National Army

## Youth and Revolutionary Voices

-** Bhagat Singh, Sukhdev, Rajguru**: Inspired youth through revolutionary sacrifice and socialist ideals
-** Hindustan Socialist Republican Association**: Provided alternative to non-violent approach
-** Subhas Chandra Bose**: Forward Bloc and INA represented militant nationalism
-** Student movements**: Boycotted educational institutions, participated in protests nationwide
-** Azad Hind Fauj**: United Indians across religious and regional lines under nationalist banner

## Religious and Cultural Integration

-** Maulana Abul Kalam Azad**: Promoted secular nationalism through religious scholarship
-** Khan Abdul Ghaffar Khan**: Led Khudai Khidmatgar movement in NWFP with non-violent principles
-** Ashfaqullah Khan**: Revolutionary who worked with Hindu colleagues, symbolizing unity
-** Aligarh Muslim University students**: Active participants despite separate electorate debates
-** Jamiat Ulema-e-Hind**: Religious organization supporting unified India

## Regional and Linguistic Contributions

-** Non-Brahmin Movement (South India)**: E.V. Ramasamy integrated social reform with nationalism
-** Tebhaga Movement (Bengal)**: Connected peasant struggles with freedom fight
-** Praja Mandal movements**: Democratic movements in princely states joined national struggle
-** Linguistic reorganization demands**: Cultural nationalism strengthened political movement
-** Tribal participation**: Birsa Munda's legacy continued through tribal support to Gandhi

The Gandhian phase successfully created an inclusive nationalist movement where diverse voices - from peasants to intellectuals, women to youth, and different religious communities - found common ground in the struggle for** Swaraj**, establishing the foundation for India's democratic and pluralistic future.

